(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-history-physical-documentation-quiz"]={"title":"History and Physical Documentation — Physical Diagnosis 1 Exam 3","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Compared with a history and physical, the progress note is:","choices":["Broader, because it must restate the entire presenting history","More focused","Identical in scope but written in a different section order","Longer, because it accumulates every previous day's entries"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the progress note as a more focused document than a history and physical. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What does the history of present illness contain, according to the lecture?","choices":["The three-generation account of relatives and their health status","A head-to-toe survey covering every body system in full detail","A ranked list of potential diagnoses with supporting evidence","What the patient says about their chief complaint"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines it as what the patient says about the chief complaint, with symptom description, the seven attributes, pain assessment, and pertinent positives and negatives drawn from other history components. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which details does the lecture require for each medication?","choices":["Dosage, route, frequency, indication, and compliance","Manufacturer, cost, pharmacy, and insurance coverage","Only the drug names, since doses change frequently anyway","Prescribing clinician, start date, and planned stop date"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture requires all five, and includes prescription, over-the-counter, and vitamins or supplements. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which element belongs in the plan rather than the assessment?","choices":["Referrals","The final diagnosis reached after history, examination and testing","Chronic conditions that also require ongoing assessment or treatment","Concurrent diagnoses identified alongside the presenting problem"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The plan covers disposition, testing, treatment, referrals, patient education, and follow-up; the assessment is a simple list of diagnoses. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does the lecture require for a pharmacologic entry in the plan?","choices":["The drug class and an alternative agent if the first fails","A prescription number and the dispensing pharmacy","Only the drug name, since dosing is set by the pharmacist","Indications, drug name, dosage, route, and frequency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these, and its worked example gives drug, dose, route, frequency, duration, and indication together. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How many differential diagnoses does the lecture suggest aiming for?","choices":["Exactly one, so that the plan stays focused","At least ten, to demonstrate breadth of reading","Three or more","Two, one common and one dangerous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to try to have three or more, with the first being the most likely. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which topic does the lecture place in the social history?","choices":["Age-appropriate cancer screenings together with their results","Immunizations and any childhood illnesses","Safety","The reaction and severity associated with each known allergy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The social history covers substance use, diet and caffeine, exercise, occupation and socioeconomic status, travel, sexual history, support systems, stresses, safety, and life experiences. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does the lecture require alongside each past medical history entry?","choices":["The name of the facility where the care was delivered","A copy of the discharge summary for every hospitalization","Specific dates and timelines","Confirmation that the patient consented to each treatment given"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks for specific dates and timelines across major illnesses, surgeries, hospitalizations, childhood illnesses, immunizations, and screenings. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the lecture identify as the value of validation as a technique?","choices":["It confirms the accuracy of the facts the patient has given","It signals that the interview is moving to a new section","It invites the patient to keep expanding, using their own last words as the prompt","It helps the patient feel their emotions are legitimate and understandable"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the lecture's definition of validation. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about your signature on a note?","choices":["It records only that you were physically present at some point during the encounter","It may be added by a supervising clinician on your behalf","It is required only for inpatient documentation","This is a legal document, and your signature makes you responsible for the accuracy of the content"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture closes on exactly this point. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which item does the lecture list under technical issues?","choices":["Difficulty following the progression of the physical examination through the note","Inconsistency","Describing a finding only as normal without any supporting detail","Confusion between whether the document is a history and physical or a progress note"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Technical issues cover missing components, terminology, word errors, and inconsistency. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"In the lecture's worked differential for painful urination, why is pyelonephritis judged less likely?","choices":["Because the urinalysis was negative for nitrates","Because the patient reported no prior history of any urinary tract infection","Because the patient is not sexually active","Because the patient has none of the signs of ascending infection such as fever, chills, or flank pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The example lists fever, chills, flank pain, costovertebral angle pain, abdominal pain, and nausea or vomiting as absent. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"The lecture warns about directing the conversation too tightly. What is the stated risk?","choices":["The encounter will exceed the time allocated for the visit","The patient's story is affected, which can lead to omissions and errors","The clinician will be unable to complete the review of systems","The patient will refuse to answer any further questions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to direct the conversation but not control it in a way that affects the patient's story, because that can cause omissions and errors. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"For which setting does the lecture say progress notes are usually used?","choices":["Daily evaluation of hospitalized inpatients","Initial evaluation of a new outpatient referral","Emergency department triage before a clinician review","Annual preventive visits in primary care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the progress note as a standardized way to document encounters, usually for daily evaluation of hospitalized inpatients. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the order in which information is obtained versus documented?","choices":["Information is not always obtained in the order it needs to be documented","Information must be obtained in exactly the order it will be written","The note should be written in the order the patient volunteered facts","Documentation order is left entirely to individual preference"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes documentation has guidelines for organizing information while the interview is more freeform, usually moving from general to more specific. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Beyond naming the allergen, what does the lecture require be documented for allergies?","choices":["The date the allergy was first diagnosed by a clinician","The reaction and its severity","Whether a substitute medication was tolerated afterwards","The result of any formal allergy testing performed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture requires the reaction and severity, covering medication, food, and environmental allergies, and says to verify at every appointment. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the lecture recommend about body language during a first encounter?","choices":["Take notes continuously so nothing the patient says is missed","Remain seated behind a desk to maintain professional distance","Open posture and appropriate eye contact, with distractions avoided","Mirror the patient's posture exactly throughout the interview"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs these with making a good first impression and learning about patients rather than only their problems. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which question appears on the lecture's checklist for reviewing a completed note?","choices":["Is the tone professional and does the note convey neutrality?","Has the note been countersigned by a second clinician?","Does the note fit within a single printed page?","Have all abbreviations been expanded in a glossary?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The checklist asks about organisation, whether the picture is clear, whether pertinent positives and negatives are specifically described, whether anything is missing, whether it is too wordy, and about professional neutral tone. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which element does the lecture list as part of the plan?","choices":["The seven attributes of the presenting symptom","The three-generation family tree","Disposition, meaning inpatient or outpatient","The reliability of the source of the history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists disposition, testing, treatment (pharmacologic and non-pharmacologic), referrals, patient education, and follow-up. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"How does the lecture say family history should be structured?","choices":["Only first-degree relatives, listed alphabetically by name","Only relatives with a confirmed genetic diagnosis","Three generations, listed oldest to youngest, with age and health status","Two generations, listed youngest to oldest, with cause of death"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture specifies three generations oldest to youngest, with age, living or deceased status, health status and diseases, to identify possible genetic patterns. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What does the acronym structure of the progress note stand for, and how does it compare to a full history and physical?","choices":["Symptoms, observations, analysis, prescription; broader than a history and physical","Subjective, objective, assessment, plan; more focused than a history and physical","Subjective, objective, assessment, plan; identical in scope to a history and physical","Source, onset, aggravators, palliators; used only in outpatient clinics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the progress note as a standardized way to document encounters, usually for daily evaluation of hospitalized inpatients, and more focused than a full history and physical. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the physical examination within the objective portion of a progress note?","choices":["A full head-to-toe examination repeated identically each day","Omitted entirely, on the basis that it was already documented in the history and physical","Limited strictly to the vital signs","Focused and detailed, covering systems pertinent to the complaint and positive history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls for a focused, detailed examination of systems pertinent to the chief complaint and positive history, alongside updated vitals and labs. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which list matches the seven attributes of a symptom given in the lecture?","choices":["Onset, duration, severity, frequency, laterality, radiation, relief","Location, quality, quantity or severity, timing, onset, palliative or provocative factors, associated manifestations","Site, character, radiation, associations, timing, exacerbating and relieving factors, and overall severity","Position, pattern, precipitant, progression, pain score, prior episodes, plan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the seven attributes the lecture names for describing a symptom in the history of present illness. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the stated purpose of documenting pertinent negatives in the history of present illness?","choices":["They record findings that the patient could not recall clearly","They demonstrate that a full review of systems was completed","They shorten the note by replacing the review of systems entirely","They help rule out alternative diagnoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs them with pertinent positives, which argue for a diagnosis, while negatives help rule out alternatives. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"According to the lecture, guided questioning should move in which direction?","choices":["From focused questions toward broad open-ended ones","From written intake questionnaires toward spoken follow-up questions later in the visit","From general toward specific, and from open-ended toward focused","From family history toward the chief complaint"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines guided questioning as moving from general to specific and from open-ended to focused. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why does the lecture tell you to avoid terms such as \"normal\" or \"appropriate\" in exam documentation?","choices":["Because you should describe findings in enough detail that another provider reaches the same conclusion","Because those particular words are reserved for use in the assessment section of the note","Because insurers will not reimburse notes containing them","Because they are abbreviations of longer clinical phrases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls these vague terms and conclusions, and asks for description detailed enough that other providers would come to the same conclusion. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In the lecture's worked plan for a urinary infection, what is documented about follow-up?","choices":["The patient is asked to return in roughly six months for a routine scheduled review","Follow-up is deferred until culture sensitivities are reported","Follow up if symptoms do not resolve in three days, and call immediately if anything worsens","No follow-up is required for an uncomplicated infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The worked example gives both the interval and the instruction to call immediately for new, worsening, or otherwise concerning symptoms. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Besides the final diagnosis, what else belongs in the assessment?","choices":["Only conditions that were newly diagnosed at this visit","Other conditions requiring assessment or treatment, including chronic and concurrent diagnoses","Only conditions that will be treated pharmacologically","Only those conditions the patient mentioned when giving the chief complaint"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture includes chronic conditions and concurrent diagnoses alongside the final diagnosis. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What does the lecture say the social history should convey overall?","choices":["Who your patient is and what risk factors they have","A chronological account of every previous hospital admission","The patient's insurance status and ability to pay","A list of relatives with heritable conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture closes the social history slide with exactly this, after listing substance use, diet, exercise, occupation, travel, sexual history, support systems, stresses, safety, and life experiences. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Why does the lecture's closing summary say to avoid acronyms and abbreviations?","choices":["They make notes harder for billing staff to code accurately","They are prohibited by the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services","They lengthen the time required to dictate a note","They may cause misunderstandings and medical errors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly this reason and repeats the rule: when in doubt, spell it out. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
